Remove conflicting getopt() redeclaration

timg.cc hand-declares getopt() to appease clang-tidy, but does so without an
exception specifier. On old glibc that collides with the system header:

  getopt.h:151:12: error: declaration of
  'int getopt(int, char* const*, const char*) noexcept'
  has a different exception specifier

WHEN THIS BITES: only on glibc <= 2.17. There, unistd.h includes getopt.h in
__need_getopt mode, which does not declare getopt(). timg's declaration is
therefore the first one the compiler sees, and it carries no specifier. The
full <getopt.h>, included a few lines further down, then declares getopt()
__THROW (i.e. noexcept), and since C++17 makes noexcept part of the function
type, the two disagree. Newer glibc declares getopt() up front via
bits/getopt_core.h, so the redeclaration matches what came before and nothing
breaks -- which is why upstream CI on current distros is green.

WHY WE NEED IT: conda-forge builds linux against sysroot glibc 2.17
(c_stdlib_version in .ci_support/linux64.yaml, chosen for compatibility), so
every linux build hits this. Verified three ways: fails with
x86_64-conda-linux-gnu-c++ against sysroot 2.17, compiles clean with system
g++ against glibc 2.43, compiles clean with this patch applied.

Dropping the declaration is safe on all platforms: <getopt.h> is included a
few lines below and declares getopt() itself. The optarg/optind externs are
kept, as those are what clang-tidy actually needed.

This patch can be dropped if conda-forge's default sysroot ever moves past
glibc 2.17, or if upstream fixes it. Not yet reported upstream; still present
on main as of v1.6.3.

--- a/src/timg.cc
+++ b/src/timg.cc
@@ -46,7 +46,6 @@
 extern "C" {                                   //
 extern char *optarg;                           // NOLINT
 extern int optind;                             // NOLINT
-int getopt(int, char *const *, const char *);  // NOLINT
 }

 #ifdef WITH_TIMG_SIXEL
